NAME
    coretop - live htop-style dashboard of the loaded core's hardware

SYNOPSIS
    coretop [-w]

DESCRIPTION
    A live, auto-refreshing (4x/second) dashboard, styled after htop: per-
    subsystem hardware load bars, CPU registers, a sprite-capacity gauge,
    per-voice audio channel meters, a live color-RAM palette swatch, and (if
    this core has a tilemap concept) a downsampled live preview of VRAM's
    tile sheet - all in one screen, refreshing on its own clock rather than
    waiting for input. Every number comes from IDebugTarget, so a core with
    nothing to report for a given section (no hardware-load breakdown wired
    up, no fixed sprite capacity, no palettes) just makes that section
    shrink or disappear rather than showing something fake.

    On a core of the stable ABI the load bars are the phases the core times
    and names in its machine info; a core that times none shows one bar,
    'frame', the engine's own timing of the whole frame. Each is a share of
    one frame at the machine's own rate.

    Load bars are colored green/yellow/red under 60% / 60-85% / over 85%,
    the same rough 'getting busy' convention real htop's CPU bars use.

    Press Ctrl+C to exit - unlike everywhere else in this shell, Ctrl+C is
    read as a key here (via Console.TreatControlCAsInput) rather than
    raising a process-level interrupt, restored to normal the moment
    coretop exits. Needs a REAL interactive terminal, same as 'nano' and
    for the same reason - refuses cleanly rather than trying to draw
    anywhere when stdin/stdout is redirected or there's no real terminal at
    all (a piped script, a 'source'd file, a headless test).

    In EmuSen.Mistress's own console window (a TextBox, not a real
    terminal - the above wouldn't work there at all), 'coretop' is replaced
    with a windowed equivalent instead: a real, non-blocking Avalonia window
    showing the same data as actual widgets and live images (the palette and
    VRAM tile sheet render as real pictures there, not ANSI blocks) that
    refreshes on its own timer while gameplay keeps running - Ctrl+C doesn't
    apply there, just close the window.

    -w  Open the same dashboard in a separate window instead of taking over
        the terminal, so you can dismiss it and keep playing. In
        EmuSen.Mistress, coretop is already windowed by default, so -w is
        accepted and simply has no additional effect there. In EmuSen.Hotaru
        (a Raylib console build with no window of its own to reuse), -w spins
        up a small dedicated Avalonia UI thread the first time it's used and
        opens a real second OS window from it, entirely separate from the
        Raylib game window and its main loop - gameplay keeps running behind
        it. Closing that window is -w's equivalent of Ctrl+C: it's the whole
        'stop watching coretop' action, nothing else to press. If the current
        frontend has no window to open at all, -w fails cleanly with
        "not supported by this frontend" rather than falling back to the
        terminal dashboard.

EXAMPLES
    coretop
    coretop -w
